<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      FS.GG.Templates#384 (root cause established by #380). `RestoreLockedMode` above
      pins each package's identity, version and CONTENT HASH — so it is only
      enforceable if exactly one archive can ever answer for a given id+version.

      The F# SDK appends its own bundled folder feed to the restore sources:

        sdk/<ver>/FSharp/Microsoft.FSharp.NetSdk.targets:69
          <PropertyGroup Condition=" '$(DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder)' != 'true' ">
            <RestoreAdditionalProjectSources>...;$(_FSharpCoreLibraryPacksFolder)</...>

      That folder ships one archive — `FSharp.Core.10.1.302.nupkg`, 3051536 bytes — at
      the same version nuget.org publishes as 3066531 bytes. Two byte-different
      packages, one id and version, both reachable in the same restore; whichever one a
      given restore reaches gets its `contentHash` written into the lock, and a later
      restore that reaches the other reports the disagreement as

        error NU1403: Package content hash validation failed for FSharp.Core.10.1.302.
                      The package is different than the last restore.

      which is #380 exactly, observed in the sibling fs-gg-fable-game template.

      BE PRECISE ABOUT THIS WORKSPACE'S EXPOSURE, because it is not identical to
      fs-gg-fable-game's and pretending otherwise would make this comment untrue. These
      projects target netstandard2.1 and take FSharp.Core 4.7.2 transitively through
      Fable.Core, so the version the SDK folder ships is not the version resolved TODAY
      (measured: `packages.lock.json` beside both projects records FSharp.Core 4.7.2).
      This property is therefore a forward guardrail here rather than a repair of a
      live collision: the day Fable.Core's floor moves, or a project retargets, the
      exposure arrives silently and locked mode would enforce whichever archive won.
      Setting it now costs nothing this workspace uses — every package it restores
      already comes from nuget.org — and it keeps all four FS-GG workspace templates
      answering the question the same way.

      `<clear />` in NuGet.config cannot close this: the folder arrives through
      RestoreAdditionalProjectSources (MSBuild), not through NuGet configuration, so
      nothing in NuGet.config removes it. This property is the lever that does.
    -->
    <DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder>true</DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder>

    <!--
      The second half of #380, and the half that source pinning cannot reach. Unlike the
      property above, this one is load-bearing for THIS workspace today.

      `DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder` decides which SOURCE is consulted — but only for
      a package that must be fetched at all. NuGet's global packages folder is keyed by
      id+version alone, so once `fable.core/5.2.0/` or `fsharp.core/4.7.2/` is extracted
      there, every later restore on that machine uses it and validates the lock against the
      `contentHash` recorded in its `.nupkg.metadata`. Whichever build reached that folder
      FIRST decides which archive lives there, and nothing a product declares can overrule
      an entry that already exists.

      Measured both ways in #380 on FSharp.Core 10.1.302, where two archives are known to
      exist: a machine whose shared folder holds nuget.org's copy restores green; a machine
      whose shared folder holds the SDK's copy fails with NU1403 — same product, same lock,
      same source pinning, opposite result. That is not a property this workspace can fix by
      naming a source, and it is why a CI runner reds while a developer container passes.
      The mechanism is generic over package id: it is not a claim about FSharp.Core.

      So the workspace keeps its packages to itself. `RestorePackagesPath` gives it a
      private package folder, which makes the committed `contentHash` beside it enforceable
      on ANY machine regardless of what that machine's shared folder happens to hold. This
      is the cost of a lock that actually means something: packages are not shared with
      other checkouts, so a cold build downloads its own copies. A CI cache should key on
      `packages.lock.json` and cache `.nuget/packages` in the workspace.

      Removing this line does not fail loudly — it makes the workspace green on machines
      that agree with the lock and red on machines that do not, which is exactly the
      intermittent NU1403 #380 spent its life chasing.
    -->
    <RestorePackagesPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory).nuget/packages</RestorePackagesPath>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
